# Muze Next Step

## ☐ Resume Discussion QA From Members

Start the next manual-testing/fix session from the active-room Members flow. Deeply test
the member list and opening member profiles from it, including Back/Close behavior,
exact-source returns, bottom-keyboard mode restoration, stale inline actions, active-room
controls, rapid taps, both languages, and unnecessary Telegram sends/deletes. Preserve
identity-bound and destructive action safety while fixing the UX bugs found there.

☐ Start a controlled private beta with 20–50 users.

Watch onboarding completion, Top 10 activity, discussion joins/invites, D1/D7 return,
errors, bot heartbeat, and Star ledger reconciliation. Keep optional Star spending
switches conservative and fix observed friction before adding features.

Later product ideas and "do not implement now" notes live in `plan.todo`.

## ☑ Faster Hybrid Navigation With Bottom-Keyboard Hubs

Implemented the evolved development contract after end-to-end manual QA:

- Stable hubs, profile/settings/edit pickers, media managers and suggestions,
  discussion lists/create steps, People/Saved lists, and exact-source profile returns use
  server-scoped bottom reply keyboards.
- First-session onboarding, language selection, Interest pages with direct typed items,
  the optional taste nudge, and completion destinations also use server-scoped bottom
  modes without deleting or editing earlier prompts. Inline controls remain only when the payload must stay
  bound to a Telegram message or exact identity/price/nonce, including share/copy URLs,
  destructive account actions, member moderation, invitation joins, and transactional
  Star confirmations.
- `navigationMode` is durable because exact page, filter, source, profile, draft, and
  quoted-payment context must survive restarts and two devices. Visible Telegram
  keyboards are never trusted; stale or manually typed labels are ignored.
- Ordinary navigation retains chat history and performs no footprint send, Telegram
  delete/edit cleanup, or navigation-card tracking. Legacy tracked IDs are forgotten
  without a Telegram API call. Only consumed unsafe/indexed inline cards receive
  flow-specific cleanup.
- Commands, deep links, active-room controls, language changes, temporary input modes,
  rapid taps, and payment safety keep precedence over reply-keyboard resolution.

Rollout completed in the requested order: resolver and guard tests, global menu,
Discussions, People, Favorites/media, then the broader profile/discussion/list conversion.
The scripted navigation journey is covered by deterministic transition-operation tests;
temporary instrumentation was removed after confirming ordinary converted transitions
use one destination send, zero Telegram deletes/markup edits, and zero navigation-card
writes. The legacy lifecycle required a destination send plus footprint, a prior-card
delete/disable, and card clear/track writes on each contextual transition. Local test
handler timings are network-free and therefore not a substitute for production Telegram
latency; production slow-update metrics remain the source for median/p95 monitoring.

Validation: translation catalog, full unit suite, integration suite, and changed-file
diff checks are required to remain green before commit.
